WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:03.234
[ Device beeping ]

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:05.167
[ Creature roaring ]

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.868
[ Beeping continues ]

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.868
[ Tense music plays ]

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:15.734
♪♪

00:00:15.734 --> 00:00:22.734
[ Creature roaring ]

00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:25.701
[ Thunder crashes ]

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:28.167
[ Creature growls ]

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:33.234
♪♪

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:34.901
[ Creature roars ]

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.234
-Hey, everybody.
Welcome back to "Late Night"!

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:38.868
[ Cheers and applause ]
We're here with Adam Driver.

00:00:38.868 --> 00:00:41.567
That was a clip from "65."

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:45.601
This film take place
65 million years in the past.

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:46.701
-Yes.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:48.400
-Where do you shoot
a movie like that?

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.767
Like, where are we living now
that best looks like that?

00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.000
-Oddly, Louisiana.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.434
-Okay, good.
That's no comment on Louisiana.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.267
-Right, right, no.
-A very robust film --

00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.167
-Yeah, yeah, not the red-light
district or the French Quarter.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.367
[ Laughter ]
-The French Quarter, yeah.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.367
-Yeah, oddly, the French Quarter
in New Orleans.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.667
No, it's, like in the swamps
out near New Orleans.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.501
-Okay.
-That's what they --

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:11.300
Or at least
I was told by somebody

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.601
that it was the closest thing
to prehistoric Earth.

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:16.367
Then we went to Oregon.
-Okay.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:19.567
-Yeah, to shoot the big trees,
the redwoods.

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.267
-Oh, very good.
So, ultimately, if you can

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:23.701
combine Louisiana and Oregon,

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:25.000
you basically have
65 million years ago.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.100
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.968
Or I guess the redwoods
are more North --

00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:30.067
Eh, somewhere in Oregon.
I can't remember where we were.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.868
Actually, I should remember
where we were.

00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:33.067
We were there for a long time.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:34.267
But, yes, the combination
of those things.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.033
-But you were so deep
in character, like,

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:37.868
it wasn't Oregon to you.
-Exactly, thank you, thank you.

00:01:37.868 --> 00:01:39.100
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.968
It was 65 million years ago.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.267
-How could you possibly
remember something

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:45.100
that happened that long ago?
-Right, right, right.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.801
-You also -- we were just
talking during the break

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:50.701
that you got to work
with two directors who've --

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.133
I was gonna say I love,
but I think everybody loves --

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.234
Michael Mann.
-Yeah.

00:01:53.234 --> 00:01:56.300
-And Francis Ford Coppola.
-Yeah.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.667
-When you go in --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.901
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:03.334
-Do you go into things
like that differently?

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:04.901
Are you thinking --
Or do you try to

00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:06.400
get yourself out of your head
and know it's, like,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.701
the same kind of work
you want to do on every movie?

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:10.100
Even when
you're working with legends.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.133
-I think it's a bit of both.
They also are people

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.801
who are so disarming
that you don't really --

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.000
the legend of them or what you
relate to them and their movies

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.033
and how, you know, how much
their films have meant to you,

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:24.400
where you replay moments,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.133
it kind of goes away
really quickly.

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:29.367
'Cause they're just, you know,
they're people who are just --

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:32.234
So you pretty -- you find
a rhythm, I think, easily.

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:35.033
Most -- with directors like that
who are really good,

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:37.701
they are really disarming
because they want the best

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.100
out of what it is
that you give them.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.000
-Right.
I think they would obviously

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.467
know from their long careers
that if you walk around

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:45.701
like a legend, they're not gonna
get the best work out of people.

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.133
-Yeah, you drop your movies
on the floor.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.234
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.467
No, they want --

00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.934
You know,
they want the best out of you.

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:54.734
So there really can't be a
distance between you and them.

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:58.334
And they bring their ev--
so much of themselves to a set,

00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:00.868
and you, I think,
have to do the same.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:04.467
-You -- I know you put a lot of
work before you start a movie

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:07.100
and then the work you put into
a movie while you're filming it.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.033
But I've also heard that as,
soon as it's over,

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:11.133
you like to move on.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:12.968
-I mean, I will say,
I'm not doing

00:03:12.968 --> 00:03:14.634
the same kind of work here,
but we do the same thing, right?

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:17.133
I'm never like, "I'm gonna
go watch Monday's show."

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:18.634
It's like you move --
-Right, right.

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:20.234
-Is that why --
Do you just feel like

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:22.567
it's important because you don't
want the previous work

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:25.734
to get in the way
or to clutter the next?

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:28.767
-Maybe that,
but it's also the --

00:03:28.767 --> 00:03:30.267
I can't control it.
It's not my movie

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:33.167
that I'm directing, and acting
is so much a service industry.

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:36.834
So you go in and do as much
as you possibly can with what --

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:39.133
the time and budget
and the director, you know,

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:41.100
and all of the elements
kind of allow,

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.634
and then I try to -- It's like
a protective mechanism --

00:03:43.634 --> 00:03:45.601
just move on to the next thing
and try not to think about it

00:03:45.601 --> 00:03:47.033
'cause I can't control it.

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:49.934
And I don't want to get involved
in trying to tell someone else

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:51.434
about what
their movie should be.

00:03:51.434 --> 00:03:54.701
If there's an inherent trust
with them where you're like,

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:58.234
"This is your film," you know,
"I'm fulfilling one role of it,

00:03:58.234 --> 00:04:00.667
and whatever you want
to make it, that's up to you,"

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:03.834
then I kind of don't want to get
involved, you know, emotionally

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:06.467
with "Why'd you pick that take?"
-It's a very Zen thing.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:07.868
Did you find that
from the beginning,

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:09.767
that idea of just letting go
when you're done?

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:12.100
Or did you have
to find your way to it?

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.968
-A bit of both.
I did a lot of theater

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:15.567
beforehand where
you can't take it with you,

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.400
obviously, every night,
and it only exists

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.167
how you felt about it
when you did it.

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:23.667
And to me, that was enough
of a good yardstick

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:25.534
when we're shooting
to know if it's going well.

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:27.601
I'm like, "Okay. I know what
it feels like internally.

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:30.067
I shouldn't worry about
what it looks like externally."

00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:33.434
But then I saw some of the
things I did and I hated them.

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:34.801
[ Laughter ]

00:04:34.801 --> 00:04:36.968
And you wind up putting
not only yourself through this

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:39.501
kind of really boring, like,
you know,

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:42.267
overpretentious kind of thing
of asking --

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.701
you know, of asking
everybody else around it.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:47.400
And then you kind of got to
watch it four times

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.334
to divorce that it's you
who's doing it, you know?

00:04:50.334 --> 00:04:53.000
To really, "Okay.
I remember what we were doing,

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.133
why I made that choice."

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:55.467
So, it's like,
it's just exhausting.

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:58.434
I try to just, like, do everyone
around me a favor

00:04:58.434 --> 00:04:59.834
by letting it go
as much as possible.

00:04:59.834 --> 00:05:01.400
[ Laughter ]
-Very selfless.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.133
-Yeah. Well...
-This is obviously --

00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:07.000
This movie feels very unique
to the work you've done

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.434
up to this point.
Are there other --

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:09.601
Is that sort of
what you're looking for?

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:11.133
Is there something out there --

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.234
Because, again, I feel like
you've had a lot of diversity

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:14.567
in the sort of things
you've done.

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:16.133
Is there something left
that you really want to do?

00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:18.801
The kind of movie
or kind of role?

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:21.501
-Um, yeah.

00:05:21.501 --> 00:05:25.000
Anything that's different
always interests me.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.868
And the people that are
making it is a big part of it.

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:29.400
Big budgets or small budget.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.634
I know it's like
a very cliché answer,

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:34.934
but the older you get,
I feel like,

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:36.934
then you kind of want
to be home more,

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:39.300
and things that are interesting
to you before

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.067
don't really interest you now.

00:05:41.067 --> 00:05:43.567
So, I just want
to be home and not --

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:45.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.067
This is where
this answer is going.

00:05:46.067 --> 00:05:47.234
That's not what I mean to say.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:49.000
[ Laughter ]
"I don't want to work."

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.701
-Your agent's gonna be like,
"Adam, baby!

00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:52.267
What are you doing?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.267 --> 00:05:54.701
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Well, I will tell you that I --

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:57.434
-Anything is really -- anything
that's gonna expect it to be.

00:05:57.434 --> 00:05:59.200
Not to answer --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.434
Trying to dig my way
out of this answer.

00:06:01.434 --> 00:06:03.901
Something that's funny.
You know, do the opposite.

00:06:03.901 --> 00:06:06.367
I'll play the comet
in the movie next.

00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:07.501
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.501 --> 00:06:09.634
That chases the dinosaur.

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:12.601
Help me out of this answer.
-I'm just gonna say, that I --

00:06:12.601 --> 00:06:14.000
You know,
there are certain actors

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.667
that when they choose a role,
I have confidence that,

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:17.901
you know,
it's the right thing.

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:19.367
And you've always
been someone for that.

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:20.567
I'm always excited
to see what you choose.

00:06:20.567 --> 00:06:22.133
-That's very nice.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:22.133 --> 00:06:24.534
-Thanks for being here, man.
It's always such a pleasure.

00:06:24.534 --> 00:06:27.634
Adam Driver. "65" in theaters
everywhere this Friday.

00:06:27.634 --> 00:06:29.300
We'll be right back
with Adam Brody.

